.TH SHIMBACK 1 "2026-09-12" "shimback 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shimback \- run a primary command, transparently fall back to another
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shimback add
.I name
[\fB\-s\fR \fIsource\fR] [\fB\-\-source\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|.
\fB\-f\fR \fIfallback\fR [\fB\-\-fallback\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|.
[\fB\-\-policy\fR \fIexit\-code\fR|\fIheuristic\fR|\fIexit\-code\-match\fR|\fIroute\-args\fR|\fIrewrite\fR]
[\fB\-\-error\-pattern\fR \fIp\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-exit\-code\fR \fIcode\fR]\|.\|.\|.
[\fB\-\-route\-arg\fR \fIarg\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-strip\-matched\-args\fR]
[\fB\-\-rewrite\fR \fIfrom\fR=\fIto\fR]\|.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-diagnostic\fR]
.br
.B shimback remove
[\fB\-y\fR]
.I name
(alias:
.BR rm )
.br
.B shimback init
.br
.B shimback list
[\fB\-\-full\fR]
(alias:
.BR ls )
.br
.B shimback doctor
[\fBfix\fR]
.br
.B shimback install
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIdir\fR]
[\fB\-\-shell\fR \fIshell\fR[,\fIshell\fR]\|.\|.\|. | \fB\-\-all\fR]
.br
.B shimback uninstall
[\fB\-\-prefix\fR \fIdir\fR] [\fB\-\-full\fR]
.br
.B shimback \-\-help
|
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shimback
is a small, dependency\-free command\-line shim: it wraps a command name
(e.g.
.IR sed )
with a
.B source
binary to run and a
.B fallback
binary to transparently retry with if the source doesn't work out.
.PP
.B add
creates a symlink named
.I name
pointing at the
.B shimback
binary itself, inside a dedicated shim directory that's prepended to
.BR PATH .
When
.I name
is run, the OS finds that symlink first;
.B shimback
inspects
.IR argv[0] ,
sees it was invoked as
.I name
rather than
.BR shimback ,
looks up
.IR name 's
configuration, and runs the source command with the given arguments. If the
source command fails (the exact condition depends on the shim's policy, see
.B POLICIES
below),
.B shimback
transparently re\-runs the same arguments against the fallback command
instead. If the source succeeds, its output is passed through as if
.B shimback
weren't there at all.
.PP
A failed trial run of the source command is designed to be invisible: its
stdout and stderr are captured, not streamed live, and are discarded
entirely if a fallback is triggered \(em nothing about the failed attempt
reaches the terminal unless the shim explicitly opts into a one\-line
diagnostic (see
.B \-\-diagnostic
below).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "add " name " [\-s " source "] \-f " fallback " [options...]"
Create or update a shim named
.IR name ,
wrapping
.I source
(or, if
.B \-s
is omitted, whatever
.I name
resolves to fresh on
.B PATH
at dispatch time, skipping shimback's own shim directory) with
.I fallback
under the given policy.
.B \-f
is required for every policy except
.IR rewrite ,
which never uses a fallback at all. Both
.B \-s
and
.B \-f
accept a path or a bare command name (looked up on
.B PATH
once, at
.B add
time, the same way a shell would find it); either way the resolved absolute
path is what gets frozen into the config. Refuses to create a shim where
source and fallback resolve to the same binary, or where either resolves
back to the
.B shimback
binary itself (a cycle \(em since every shim symlink points at the same
binary, this also catches a bare name that happens to resolve to another
shim). Also ensures the shim directory is on
.B PATH
for the current shell, via an idempotent marker\-block injection into its
startup file (on zsh,
.I ~/.zshrc.local
instead of
.I ~/.zshrc
when the former exists, since that's what most zsh setups source for
machine\-local overrides).
.IP
.B \-\-source\-arg
and
.B \-\-fallback\-arg
(each repeatable) attach fixed, baked\-in arguments to source/fallback,
independent of policy and of each other \(em whichever one actually runs
gets its own extra arguments prepended before whatever the shim was
invoked with, every time. This lets a shim double as a regular alias with
flags built in, e.g.
.B \-\-source\-arg\ \-l\ \-\-source\-arg\ \-t\ \-\-source\-arg\ \-r\ \-\-source\-arg\ \-a\ \-\-source\-arg\ \-h
on a shim wrapping
.IR ls ,
the same shape as a shell alias like
.IR "alias cools='ls -ltrah'" .
Under
.IR rewrite ,
.B \-\-source\-arg
values land before the (possibly rewritten) invocation arguments, and are
never themselves subject to rewriting.
.IP
Run at an interactive terminal with required information missing (no
.IR name ,
or no
.IR fallback ,
or a policy missing the option it needs), an interactive wizard fills it
in instead of failing: one page per field (including
.B \-\-source\-arg
/
.B \-\-fallback\-arg
as their own optional list pages), Enter to confirm and continue
(skipping an optional one if left blank), Left/Right arrows to revisit
and edit earlier pages (Right only as far as a page already reached),
Esc or Ctrl\-C to abort without writing anything. Whatever was already
given on the command line is skipped straight to the first missing page,
but stays reachable via Left.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback add sed \-f /usr/bin/sed
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "remove [\-y] " name
Remove
.IR name 's
symlink and config entry. Also available as
.BR rm .
Leaves the
.B PATH
injection in the shell startup file alone, since other shims (or a future
.BR add )
may still need it. If
.I name
doesn't match any configured shim, prints the error first, then, right
after it, a "did you mean" hint (in yellow, when stderr is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset) for whichever configured name is closest by edit (Levenshtein)
distance, scaled to that name's length so an unrelated one is never
suggested \(em no external tool involved, and any single\-character typo
(dropped, inserted, or substituted) is caught, not just a dropped one.
The same hint appears for an unrecognized top\-level command.
.IP
.B \-y
(or
.BR \-\-yes )
skips the hint and acts on it automatically, but only when unambiguous:
if exactly one configured name is the closest match, that one is removed
instead; if two or more tie for closest, it falls back to the plain
error and hint, same as without the flag. A successful removal always
prints
.IR "removed '<name>'" ,
in green when stdout is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset, naming whichever shim actually got removed.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback remove sed
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B init
Detect every installed shell (zsh, bash, fish) and set up
.B PATH
in each one, not just the current shell: for zsh/bash, the same
marker\-block injection
.B add
does; for fish, a dedicated snippet dropped into
.I ~/.config/fish/conf.d/shimback.fish
(auto\-sourced by fish at startup), unioned across repeated calls the same
way and removed outright by
.BR "uninstall \-\-full" .
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback init
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "list [" \-\-full "]"
List every configured shim as a table: name, source (or
.IR auto ),
fallback, policy, and diagnostic flag. Also available as
.BR ls .
Colored when stdout is a terminal and
.B NO_COLOR
is unset.
.IP
.B \-\-full
also prints, as extra indented lines right under each shim's row,
whatever the table's columns leave out: source_args/fallback_args
(independent of policy \(em can show up for any shim), then whatever's
policy\-specific (error patterns for
.IR heuristic ,
the configured codes for
.IR exit\-code\-match ,
route args and strip\-matched\-args for
.IR route\-args ,
and each
.I from
->
.I to
pair for
.IR rewrite ).
A shim with none of the above configured has nothing extra to show.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback list \-\-full
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "doctor [" fix "]"
Check the health of the whole shimback setup: whether the shim directory
exists and is on
.BR PATH ,
whether the config file parses, and, for each shim, whether its symlink is
dead, whether its fallback/source still exist and are executable, and
whether its policy has everything it needs to ever trigger. Exits
.B 0
if everything checks out,
.B 1
otherwise.
.IP
.B fix
repairs what it safely can before reporting: a shim whose symlink is
missing entirely, or is dangling (its target no longer exists, e.g. because
the binary it pointed at has since moved or been cleaned up), gets its
symlink recreated pointing at the currently running
.B shimback
binary \(em the same thing
.B add
does when it first creates one. Never touches a symlink that still
resolves, or a path occupied by anything other than a symlink.
.IP
Also checks every shim's source (if explicit) and fallback for a cycle (a
value resolving back to the
.B shimback
binary itself \(em see
.BR add ,
above; the only way one ends up in the config, since
.B add
refuses to create one, is by hand\-editing it). When found,
.B fix
prompts interactively in the terminal for a replacement, re\-validating
each answer the same way
.B add
would and re\-prompting on an invalid one or another cycle, until it gets
something that works or is interrupted with
.BR Ctrl-C .
If stdin isn't interactive and hits EOF before being answered,
.B fix
gives up on that entry and leaves it as\-is rather than hang.
.IP
Also flags a stray PATH block: if
.I ~/.zshrc.local
exists but the shimback block is still in
.IR ~/.zshrc ,
that's a
.BR warn ,
not a failure \(em it doesn't affect the exit status, since PATH still
works either way.
.B fix
moves the block over.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback doctor fix
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "install [\-\-prefix " dir "] [\-\-shell " shell "[," shell "]\|.\|.\|. | \-\-all]"
Copy the running
.B shimback
binary to
.IR dir /bin/shimback
(default
.IR dir :
.IR \(ti/.local )
and ensure both
.IR dir /bin
and the shim directory (normally
.BR add / init 's
job) are on
.BR PATH .
Also installs this man page to
.IR dir /share/man/man1 ,
preferring a copy bundled next to the running binary and falling back to
downloading it from the shimback GitHub release matching the running
version if no local copy is found (requires
.BR curl ;
skipped with a warning, non\-fatally, if unavailable). Safe to re\-run after
building a newer version to refresh the installed copy.
.IP
By default only the
.B current
shell gets a
.B PATH
entry, like
.BR add .
.B \-\-shell
takes a comma\-separated list to target specific shells regardless of which
one is running;
.B \-\-all
means every shell
.B init
would detect as installed, fish included.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback install \-\-prefix ~/.local \-\-shell zsh,bash
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP
.BI "uninstall [\-\-prefix " dir "] [\-\-full]"
Remove every symlink
.B shimback
created in the shim directory, the
.IR dir /bin/shimback
binary installed by
.BR install ,
and the man page installed alongside it. With
.BR \-\-full ,
also delete the config file and undo the
.B PATH
setup in shell startup files (the zsh/bash marker block, and the fish
conf.d snippet); without it, the config and
.B PATH
setup are left alone.
.RS
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
shimback uninstall \-\-full
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH POLICIES
.TP
.B exit\-code
(the default) Fall back whenever the source command exits non\-zero.
.TP
.B heuristic
Only fall back when the source's stderr matches one of the shim's
configured
.B \-\-error\-pattern
values (case\-insensitive substring match). Requires at least one
.BR \-\-error\-pattern .
.TP
.B exit\-code\-match
Only fall back when the source exits with one of the shim's configured
.B \-\-exit\-code
values (repeatable, each 0\(en255). Any other non\-zero exit surfaces as\-is.
Requires at least one
.BR \-\-exit\-code .
.TP
.B route\-args
Not a fallback\-on\-failure policy: picks source or fallback
.I before
running either, based on whether any invocation argument exactly matches
one of the shim's configured
.B \-\-route\-arg
values (fallback if so, source if not), then runs only that one with live,
inherited stdio \(em no captured trial run, no retry on failure. Pass
.B \-\-strip\-matched\-args
to drop the matched argument(s) before forwarding the rest. Requires at
least one
.BR \-\-route\-arg .
.TP
.B rewrite
Not a fallback\-on\-failure policy either, and unlike every other policy,
.B \-\-fallback
is optional and never used \(em this is an alias/argument\-macro mechanism,
not a retry mechanism. Each
.BI "\-\-rewrite " from = to
rule (repeatable) is checked against every invocation argument; a match is
replaced with
.I to
before running the source, live and inherited, no capture, no retry. A
.I to
containing spaces expands into multiple forwarded arguments; an empty
.I to
drops the matched argument. Arguments matching no rule pass through
unchanged. Requires at least one
.BR \-\-rewrite .
.RS
.PP
Examples:
.RS
.nf
shimback add tmux \-s /usr/bin/tmux \-\-policy rewrite \-\-rewrite "all=ls"
shimback add ls \-s /bin/ls \-\-policy rewrite \-\-rewrite "\-\-full=\-ltrah"
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
By default, falling back leaves no trace. Pass
.B \-\-diagnostic
at
.B add
time (or set
.I diagnostic = true
in the config) to print a single line to stderr whenever that shim falls
back:
.RS
.nf
shimback: 'sed' failed; falling back to /usr/bin/sed
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/shimback/config.toml
Shim configuration (falls back to
.IR $HOME/.config/shimback/config.toml
if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset). Plain, hand\-editable TOML, managed by
.B add
and
.BR remove .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/shimback/bin
The shim directory: every shim's symlink lives here (falls back to
.IR $HOME/.local/share/shimback/bin ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 127
The shim, source, or fallback couldn't be found or isn't executable.
.TP
.B 1
A CLI or config validation error.
.TP
other
Passed straight through from whichever of source/fallback actually ran (or
128 + signal if it was killed by a signal).
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation and source:
.UR https://github.com/pnavais/shimback
.UE
.SH AUTHOR
Pablo Navais
